# Read by uwsm, not by any shell you log into. For `uwsm start -D Hyprland`
# (greetd's command) it sources ~/.config/uwsm/env, then env-hyprland (yours),
# then every file in env-hyprland.d/ -- this is one -- with `.` under sh, and
# keeps only what is EXPORTED: a bare assignment here is read and then dropped.
#
# ERGON-64. Chromium picks where an Electron app keeps its encryption key from
# the desktop name, and "Hyprland" is not one it knows: Discord then stores its
# login token unencrypted and VS Code keeps secrets in memory only. With no
# desktop name it recognises, Chromium checks whether GNOME_DESKTOP_SESSION_ID
# EXISTS, and if it does, keeps the key in gnome-keyring through libsecret.
#
# Empty, and it has to stay empty. Chromium only asks whether it is set; every
# xdg-utils script reads a non-empty value as "this is GNOME", and `xdg-open
# <dir>` -- zsh's `o` -- then fails looking for a terminal GLib does not know.
#
# Here, because every other place was measured and fails:
#   environment.d        systemd's generator rejects an empty value, in the journal only
#   hypr/common/env.lua  hl.env reaches systemd and D-Bus as a queued exec, racing autostart
#   XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP  adding :GNOME also hands every GLib app's proxy lookup to GNOME
#   per-app flags        Discord's launcher reads none, and one launch without the flag
#                        leaves what was stored undecryptable
#
# Side effect: Chromium now takes its proxy from gsettings org.gnome.system.proxy
# instead of http_proxy/https_proxy. Nothing here sets a proxy, so nothing moves
# today; one exported later would be ignored by every Electron app.
#
# Half of the fix. With the login keyring locked, the first Electron app waits
# behind an "Unlock Login Keyring" dialog; the pam_gnome_keyring lines
# bin/provision-arch.sh adds to /etc/pam.d/greetd unlock it at login.
export GNOME_DESKTOP_SESSION_ID=
